{"qid":"Q914898","names":["Kingdom of Khotan"],"title":"Kingdom of Khotan","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Kingdom of Khotan was a Buddhist Saka-speaking Iranian state in the western Tarim Basin, centered on the oasis city of Khotan (Yutian) along the southern Silk Road. Over more than a millennium the kingdom was a conduit for Buddhist scripture, silk technology, and jade trade between India, China, and the Iranian world; Chinese records document its relations with successive Han, Tang, and later dynasties. Khotan experienced a Tang protectorate in the 7th–8th centuries and intermittent Tibetan occupation before reasserting independence in the late 9th century. The Kara-Khanid Khanate under Yusuf Qadir Khan conquered Khotan around 1006 CE, ending its Buddhist polity and converting its population to Islam.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Khotan's oasis core is well established archaeologically and in Chinese sources; the extent of its dependencies and the precise chronology of the Kara-Khanid conquest are less certain.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Encyclopaedia Iranica: Khotan","url":"https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/khotan"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Khotan","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Khotan"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kingdom of Khotan","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Vijaya Sambhava","nativeName":"विजय सम्भव","roman":"Vijaya Sambhava","start":-100,"end":-60,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Vijaya Dharma","nativeName":"विजय धर्म","roman":"Vijaya Dharma","start":-60,"end":-20,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"ima